## Tag `survey`: what work the target needs

**What this task is for.** Code makes the run's tasks from what you write,
one per row, and no agent plans after you. So decide what must exist before
this checkpoint is served as a ModelingV2 target on the deployments the spec
states: the operations its forward calls, and whether an entry the tree
already has serves each at this checkpoint's values; its weights; its
routing; its per-sequence state; and what each later deployment adds.

**A row is the least work without which a deployment the spec states is not
served, or not judged as the spec states it.** One you leave out is work
nobody does. One beyond that, or one its worker cannot finish from its card,
holds back everything that waits for it. What else you think worth checking
(a comparison or a bound the spec does not state) is not a row: propose it
as a question on your `ready` receipt, which holds nothing. The spec is the
watching session's to widen, not yours.

How you reach that is yours. Your dispatch carries what the run measured for
you: what the shared catalog certifies on this architecture, the engine's own
model of the architecture where it registers one, the engine's limits for
this geometry, and each deployment's CUDA-graph capture batches. A config
has misled a survey in three ways: a multimodal checkpoint nests the
language model's geometry under `text_config`; a per-layer pattern is not a
sliding window; a drafter may sit under one key while its other keys name
another algorithm.

**Hand back** two files. Write them early and rewrite them: only what
stands when you write your receipt counts.

`plan.md`, for the workers who build the target: what distinguishes this
target (checkpoint, architecture, parallelism), the model's computation
steps in order with the operation that serves each and this checkpoint's
values, how the target gets past each engine limit your dispatch lists, each
deployment's benchmark and its protocol, and `## Open risks`.

`vocabulary.yaml`, which code turns into tasks:

```yaml
queue:    # one kernel task each: an operation the forward calls
  - n: 1
    mode: onboard | verify        # write an entry no one has | certify one at this checkpoint's values
    target: <category>/<name>     # a shared catalog entry; ops/<category>/<name> for one only this target calls
    depends_on: [<queue numbers>]
    deployment: <name>            # only when a later deployment first needs it
    site: <lowercase_words>       # optional: names the local variant of a shared entry
    cells: [{<key>: <value>, ...}]        # what it must certify
    optional_cells: [...]                 # what it may leave, reported not done
rows:     # every other piece of work
  - id: <lowercase-words>
    kind: weights | routing | state | feature
    outcome: <what this row is for: one observable result>
    writes: [<repository-relative files the row owns, its test among them>]
    depends_on: [<row ids, queue numbers>]
    deployment: <name>            # only when a later deployment first needs it
    needs: model                  # only when its check must build, load or serve the whole model
    required: ["<what must hold>"]
    optional: ["<what may be left, reported not done>"]
    leads: ["<a name to search for, what it does, why it may fit>"]
    check: {gpus: 0, wall: 1800}  # its check's job; left out: no device (one for `state`), 1800 s
```

Code reads these keys and holds them to their form: `n`, `mode`, `target`,
`site`, `id`, `kind`, `writes`, `depends_on`, `deployment`, `needs`, `check`.
It also refuses a row of `rows` with no `outcome`; the words are yours.
Everything else is yours to word, on either list (`cells`, `required`,
`optional`, `leads`, a `surface`, a `note`, any map of the steps you want to
keep): it is held to no form, and what you write there is what the row's
worker reads on its card.

**What code makes of it.** That form is all a survey is held to: nothing in
the flow's own source says more. A `queue` row is one kernel task; on a
shared entry it lands as the target's own variant
(`ops/<category>/<name>__<site>`, with its test), and the shared entry
stays as it is. A row of `rows` is one task whose write scope is its
`writes`, or one module and one test named for it; two rows never own the
same file. Code adds, with no row from you: the package's skeleton before
any task; each deployment's assembly, whose scope is the target's own
files, and its session. **Write no row for what CI needs** (the target's
accuracy test, its line in a test list, the anchor's row): code writes
those once the first deployment is onboarded.

A row's `kind` says what it covers: `weights`, every checkpoint key with
its transform, its sharding or the reason it is skipped; `routing`, the
target's registration, the axes it serves and why it refuses the rest;
`state`, each per-sequence state object, the manager that owns it, and its
transfer where a deployment splits context from generation; `feature`, one
behaviour the spec asks of a deployment, and its proof.

**How it is judged.** Code refuses a file it cannot make tasks from, and
says every problem at once. After that your survey is judged by what it
causes:

- **One worker works each row, from its card alone.** It tests every line
  you put under `required` or `cells` while the work after the row waits. So
  a row is one behaviour, proven by the smallest test that would fail
  without the row's work, and its cells cover each axis once from a base
  cell, at the sizes the deployments' options state (each dtype a deployment
  runs, the rows a call passes at the smallest and the largest captured
  batch, each per-rank pool size, one prefill chunk at `max_num_tokens`):
  never the product of the axes. What may be left goes under `optional`,
  which never holds a row back.
- **Each deployment is then loaded once, in a session** that shows that
  every rank ran the options it was given, that the boot prompts and the API
  answer, the score against its floor, and what the deployment's `probes`
  name. What a session measures is no row.
- **Two keys say when a row of either list runs.** With neither, before the target is
  assembled: its check can test only the row's own files. With
  `needs: model` (its check must build, load or serve the whole model),
  after its deployment's assembly and before that deployment's session. With
  the `deployment` of a later one, once the first deployment is onboarded.
  What the first deployment's own options turn on (CUDA graphs, the overlap
  scheduler) is that deployment's assembly, not a row.
- **No row names a place in the engine**, and none a launch command: a row
  says what to find, by names to search for. The only paths are `writes`.
- **A row never asks a target to call Modeling V1 model code** (your
  prompt): it wraps the kernel that code calls, or copies the code.
  Reusing what the tree has costs a row minutes and writing it again costs
  hours, so a row that writes new code is worth it only where nothing the
  tree has serves.
- **An anchor is never invented or changed**: the score a deployment's
  benchmark is held to. One that looks wrong, or a deployment that has
  none, is a question on your `ready` receipt: the survey does not wait.

If no set of rows can serve this checkpoint, write `blocked` with what you
searched: a name you did not find is not a reason, a behaviour nothing in
the tree or its libraries offers is.
